very very  laborious  to ask for us where we were writing new  table schemas writing migrations  bothering people for backfill and that  was something that just didn't work so  when we set out into our adventure of  moving everything over into bigquery  that was one of the things that was most  important to us during the build-out so  there were a couple of things that we we  did in terms of the way that we  architected the system that sort of  proven to be super super important for  us and allowed us to move much more  quickly than we were in the past so the  first one is that all of our pipeline  data just go straight into GCS seems  like kind of a simple face value thing  but it's actually super important  because what that means is that we don't  have to do any work as a data team to  get stuff into the place that it needs  to be for us to get it into bigquery if  it passes through the pipeline it's  there the second one was that all it  required for us to actually import data  is writing a JSON schema so previously  like I said we had to write these  migrations we had to bother people for  backfill now all of that's built in all  you do is write a config for what you  want your table to look like you deploy  that your data is there you can deploy  those tables at any time with a review  from your teammate you're not blocked by  the data engineering team and you get  your backfill for free which is  something that was super super crucial  for us all you do is write in a start  date as long as the log files are there  you'll get that data back so whatever  start date you set so this has been huge  for us and it's totally changed the way  that we think about our workflow and  actually getting the data that we needed  into our system so that we can service  it to our end users so once we've done  that with a couple of other things that  were super important and the biggest one  of these was actually giving analysts  and data scientists the tools they need  to build for less technical users so we  had taken a philosophy in the past where  we had actually built a lot of stuff  in-house and when we were just a  publisher that made list content that  lived on the website like I said earlier  that actually served us really well  because we had something that was custom  built to our needs but it didn't scale  very well as soon as we sort of started  publishing on all these other platforms  it became a really really big burden for  us to find a way to incorporate that new  stuff into dashboards because we were  suddenly relying on our engineering team  to help us sort of do the whole process  of how do we redesign this how do we do  this whole thing so we knew we needed a  better system that would allow us to  iterate more quickly I was like I said  this is sort of the core of the way that  we think about everything we do we  really want to be able to power that  feedback loop for our con  creators so what we ultimately landed on  was that we needed to use a third-party  solution and the one that we picked was  looker I'm sure some of you guys are  familiar with this with looker and I'm  gonna talk a little bit more about why  it works for us and why I might work for  you  so why did we choose looker like I said  one of the biggest things that we found  was gonna be huge for us was the minimal  damn overhead you do need to have a  little bit of maintenance at the initial  setup but once that's sort of in place  your data team can actually do a lot of  a heavy lifting because looker manages a  lot of the front end and you're really  just sort of managing the data modelling  piece which is something that most dated  teams can do on their own  the second thing that works really  really well for us and keep in mind  those sort of varying skill sets that I  mentioned earlier when I'm talking about  this is that it can support both a  general and a specific use case so what  does that actually mean  looker actually has created different  versions of their experience for people  who have different levels of comfort  working with data so they call this  Explorer versus dashboards they're you  know sort of different ways that you can  think about what that actually mean this  example that you're seeing here is a  dashboard so this is a very curated  experience that's someone who's a little  less comfortable working with the raw  data might see this is actually a real  dashboard that we have this is showing  you completions and results for a  specific quiz so if you're someone who's  writing quizzes for BuzzFeed comm you  want to come in you want to see what  results people were getting what they're  choosing for different questions we  actually are able to serve this alright  to you and a very curated looker  experience where all you have to do is  put in a URL so we're taking a lot of  the burden off of the end user in terms  of having to understand how that data is  structured where it comes from how to  pull it how to initial a tit they get  the answers that they need super quickly  but we can also support a much more  specific use case for someone like an  analyst maybe someone on our finance  team so liquor calls this Explorer the  way that it looks is it's essentially a  big pivot table that sits right on top  of your data warehouse you have someone  on your on your more technical data team  that comes in and sort of sets with the  modeling layer which will define the  dimensions and the measures and sort of  how those things are aggregated it's all  sequel based to a language called look  ml that's kind of a weird hybrid between  llamo and sequel but it's pretty easy to  work with and it allows us to sort of  support these more specific use cases so  if let's say our finance team wants to  come in here and cut stuff up by months  and countries and all sorts of different  things that most users don't need they  have access to do that stuff right here  in the tool and this is actually the  Explorer that undergirds this dashboard  I just showed you guys so you can see  how does the two things sort of connect  to each other um there were a couple of  other things that we found worked really  well for us when we were looking at  looker and a bunch of other tools one of  the other key ones is that it has tons  of off-the-shelf access controls so this  was important for us for a couple of  reasons first of all we have users that  are working across these different  platforms  some people might work on say Facebook  and YouTube but you might have some  people who work on just snapchat or just  Instagram and one of the things that we  found early on is that it was very  confusing and overwhelming for them to  like be able to see just everything if  that's not what they needed so this  allowed us to sort of partition off the  experience and make it a little bit more  digestible by only showing people the  things that they wanted to see here they  needed to see similarly you could  imagine this become very useful if you  have sensitive data in your instance so  the ways that a lot of other people uses  is that if they have to say revenue data  or finance data that they only want  specific teams to be able to see you can  kind of use it in the same way we've  done that a little bit but it's  something that's sort of a left less  important for the way that we think  about how this data is surface we really  like to democratize and make most things  available to most people another thing  that's great is that it's actually  version control so it's connected  directly to get this is super helpful  for the modeling layer it's also  extremely helpful when something breaks  and you're trying to figure out what  happened you can actually like chase it  back to a specific commit this has also  been really helpful because we're using  we're relying pretty heavily on a  functionality that looker has it's  called PD T's persistent derived tables  so what it allows you to do is to kind  of create your own on-the-fly  aggregations that run on a schedule and  are cached so if you say have hourly  data that you want to roll up daily or  monthly you can actually do this right  and looker it's all version control you  can sort of see how those aggregations  are working how they're changing  something that's worked really well for  us so much so that we're actually  starting to steal it into one of our own  features which I'll talk about in a  little bit and then it's also been a  great prototyping ground so we still  have our home home built tools we still  rely on those a bunch sort of as like  kind of the front page for the way that  a lot of people experience data with  Booker being kind of the deeper use case  that they have  so this has been a great place for us to  sort of test out new new data  experiences that we want to make  available to people and potentially  integrate into our existing tools which  is actually something you can see we've  done with that same quiz data that I  showed you so now we have this tool  called - bird this was the old dashboard  that I showed you about that was created  originally just to show post performance  so we've now gone and taken that more  granular quiz performance data and just  incorporated it directly into the  dashboard experience for all our quizzes  this is something that users really like  it reduces the friction they can kind of  see everything they want in one place  and getting data like that  into this experience previously would  have been something that would have  required a lot more work a lot more  effort a lot more dev hours so it's a  way that we've sort of been able to  reduce the lead time on some of these  things and also test much more quickly  right I think we would have been much  more hesitant to build something like  that knowing that it would have required  so much to have worked previously and  now we were able to kind of just plop it  right in there so I've talked a lot  about our data team and I want to talk a  little bit about the way that we think  about building for our non-technical  users because these are really the  people that are most important and  they're on the front lines so this boils  down to sort of four things that we  found in our experience and the many  iterations of building within looker the  first one and this is actually a huge  reason that we chose bigquery is that  people want answers really quickly we  sort of were the architects of our own  demise in the sense that we had been  very thoughtful about the way that we  had engineered a lot of our home built  tools so people were used to things  loading really really quickly so when we  suddenly put them in an environment  where they were hitting a data warehouse  rather than say Castlevania or a Redis  cache they were like why is this taking  so long to load I'm used to like two  seconds and suddenly I'm waiting two  minutes for things so bigquery was a way  for us to alleviate a lot of those  problems create an experience that was  much faster and much more attune to what  people were used to particularly you  know when you consider that a lot of  these people are non-technical and their  eyes kind of glaze over when you start  explaining it and then the reason that  the thing is slow is not because they  did something wrong or you did something  wrong but just because the warehouse is  taking a long time or maybe has a big  query backlog right now so that's a  place where bigquery has helped  a lot another thing we found and I sort  of alluded to this earlier is that less  is more when we first started working  with liquor we sort of were like oh my  god we can suddenly open up the whole  warehouse to everybody this is so great  and that actually was really bad because  people got into this experience and they  were like I don't know what any of this  stuff means why are there like six  different view metrics like I don't  understand the difference between a time  series like why does this number not  change at all when I add dates so we've  really sort of reeled back a lot of that  stuff tried to simplify really try to  show people sort of the bare minimum of  what they need is a starting point and  then scale up from there rather than  starting off by kind of opening up the  floodgates to that same point clearly  labeled and described fields are  everything we made a huge mistake  early on of relying on less technical  users to understand oh this is an  aggregate this is not an aggregate it  just was a big mistake and we actually  lost a lot of trust that it took us a  little while to build back so this is  something that I like can't emphasize  highly enough and as we sort of work  through a lot of these challenges we've  actually imposed a very strict Scott  style guide on our technical users that  is requiring them to do a lot of these  things because of how important we found  it to be and then the last piece sort of  in tune of what I show you guys on the  last slide we've really tried not to  fight existing workflows if we don't  have to  so if users already have a tool that  they're used to going to if there's a  way for you to sort of integrate the  different things you're doing together  that's something that we found works  super super well so now that I sort of  watch you guys through all this where  has it actually gotten us where are we  today  so this is our usage as of a couple of  weeks ago so we have hundreds of  terabytes of data across 300 plus tables  accessible to our analysts and our users  via bigquery I've been told that we  actually are just starting to cross into  into petabytes territory now with some  of the new stuff that we've been  importing we're running over a hundred  thousand queries a day so bear in mind  I've talked a lot about looker and  supporting our non-technical users but  look bigquery is also supporting all of  our different technical use cases so on  machine learning models all of the sort  of regularly scheduled jobs so you can  see looker actually is only about five  to ten percent of our total  workflow in a day but we're really  relying on bigquery super heavily for  all of our data needs and then one of  the things that we're actually super  proud of is that we have more than half  of our 1,500 employees using a looker  each month which I think really speaks  to what we've been able to accomplish  here especially when you consider the  fact that most of those users are people  that are not used to interacting with  the BI tool and are usually content  creators writers editors that sort of  thing so a little bit in terms of what's  next for us we're starting to experiment  with a new tool that you guys are gonna  hear more about today and tomorrow we  were one of the early access partners  for bigquery machine learning this is  something that we're really excited  about because it allows us to really  open up machine learning to a lot more  of our analysts whereas previously there  were a much more stringent technical  requirements in terms of who can do that  type of work this is also something  that's been developed in partnership  with looker which is really cool for us  because it allows us to sort of just  plug in and see sort of some things like  our model performance statistics right  in the looker experience that we're used  to using to that end we're actually  continuing to expand pretty heavily on  our existing recommendation systems so  this is something that actually powers a  lot of what you see on the BuzzFeed site  and even what you see on our Facebook  pages it's all happening behind the  scenes but it's stuff that you might not  realize is there so it's the way that we  optimize our feeds this is the way that  we're recommending content to our social  media editors to post on our different  Facebook pages we have about 140 of  those so you can imagine that that gets  to be a pretty complicated problem for  them so this is a place where we're  investing super heavily and and our use  of GCP and and bigquery has allowed us  to really reduce a lot of the friction  there and move much more quickly  and then we're also working on our own  materialized view solution so we  actually are just finished our version 1  launch of this and are starting to  launch on version 2 so this is the  solution that's akin to lookers  persistent derived table solution so we  really liked the idea of being able to  have sort of cache  aggregation x' that we could access but  what we didn't like was having that  locked specifically into looker because  we were running into these scenarios  where say a data scientist was working  on a model and wanted to access our  denormalized Facebook statistics and  that was only in looker and suddenly  you're hitting the looker API and then  having to do all this additional  manipulation so what we decided to do  was actually build our own solution for  this and our date engineering team who  again is sitting right there has been so  great through this process and I  personally is it as a big user of this  could not be more thrilled with the way  that it's it's turned out but this is  something that we think is going to be  really crucial for us in terms of  offering our game in terms of our data  government governance having really  consistent ways that we think about  cleaning up our data aggregating our  data so this is actually a bit of the  work that I'm sort of the most excited  about right now  [Music]
 

